            <ArticleTitle>Eruptive disseminated porokeratosis associated with corticosteroid-induced immunosuppression.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>753-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/ced.12636</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Eruptive disseminated porokeratosis (EDP) is a disease that presents clinically with sudden onset of erythematous papules and plaques, with a ridge-like border histologically represented by a cornoid lamella. We report a case of EDP occurring in a 39-year-old woman 3 days after completion of a 2-week course of oral corticosteroid therapy for an acute asthma exacerbation. The patient was treated with emollients and sun protection. Unlike the more chronic disseminated superficial (actinic) porokeratosis, EDP secondary to immunosuppression from corticosteroid therapy has very rarely been reported in the dermatological literature.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2015 British Association of Dermatologists.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
